Notes on Recode File

1.   In Mali, there was a weighted Male survey of 970 men ages 20 - 55.  The
     men were chosen from a subsample consisting of two thirds of the urban
     households, along with one third of the rural households.  If a male
     respondent's wife completed a questionnaire, selected variables from her
     questionnaire were added to the man's records in the male data file. 
     There were 970 men interviewed and 586 couples.

2.   After data editing and imputation had been completed, it was determined
     that the months of birth, for both female respondents and her children,
     as well as the month of marriage for the woman, were heaping in the
     early months of each year.  The data was re-imputed using a new
     algorithm which alternately employed year and age when the month was not
     present.  If the year was used in imputation, the flag variable was set
     to 7.  If age was used in imputation, the flag variable was set to 8.

3.   Variable V326 includes users of medicinal plants and gris-gris.

4.   Variable V329 excludes users of withdrawal and prolonged abstinence, but
     includes sources for periodic abstinence information, gris-gris, and
     medicinal plants.  It also excludes women for whom the current method
     response was "don't know" or missing.

5.   The base for H10 included all living children less than five years of
     age.

6.   For variable V607, the base is non-pregnant women who are not currently
     using contraception.

7.   The base for variable S604 is all currently married women, including one
     woman for whom marital status was missing.

8.   Variables V403, M2 and M3 all have categories "Nurse/Midwife" combined,
     in addition to two non-standard categories: 1: Hospital, PMI, Dispensary
     and 2: Matron.


Inconsistencies in the Recode File

1.   There was one case where two children were less than seven months apart.

2.   There were four cases where the women reported her last menstrual period
     as being before her last birth although she had no children.

3.   There was one case where the woman stated she was not currently using
     although she reported ever use of male sterilization.

4.   There were three cases where health and breastfeeding information was
     not identical for twins.

5.   There were 15 cases where the duration of breastfeeding and the interval
     between the last birth and the date of interview were inconsistent.

6.   There were 27 cases where the duration of amenorrhea and the interval
     between the last birth and the date of interview were inconsistent.

7.   There were 10 cases where the duration of abstinence and the interval
     between the last birth and the date of conception of a following birth
     were inconsistent.

8.   There were 20 cases where the duration of abstinence and the interval
     between the last birth and the date of interview were inconsistent.

9.   There were 35 cases where the duration of abstinence after the last
     birth and the time since last sex were inconsistent.

10.  There were 5 cases where the date of immunization was inconsistent with
     the reported date of a previous dose.

11.  There was one case where current marital status was missing.

12.  There were 4 cases where the educational level was inconsistent with the
     woman's age.